Step-by-step guide for preparing data, training a model, and running predictions.
Both single-marker and multi-marker (concatenated) workflows are covered.

────────────────────────────────────────
STEP 1 — Convert published data to CSV
────────────────────────────────────────

Published methylation files are in data/published/, one per locus.
Each file is tab-separated with three columns:
  age<float>_<sample_tag>   <sequence>   <count>

Run txt_to_csv.py to convert a file to the CSV format expected by train.py/predict.py.
Pass -p to select the CpG site positions you want (1-indexed, comma-separated).
Sequences with non-C/T characters at the selected positions are automatically discarded.

  python3 scripts/txt_to_csv.py data/published/chr6:11044843-11044997.txt -p 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9

Output is saved to:
  data/processed/chr6/11044843-11044997.csv

Repeat for every locus/marker you intend to use.


────────────────────────────────────────
STEP 2 — Register samples and split into train / validation / test
────────────────────────────────────────

Splits are stratified by age: 70/30 train+val vs test, then 70/30 train vs validation.
Split assignments are stored permanently in data/train_test_split.csv.

First time (register new samples and write split files):

  python3 scripts/split_csv.py data/processed/chr6/11044843-11044997.csv --register

Subsequent runs (only already-registered tags are written to output; registry unchanged):

  python3 scripts/split_csv.py data/processed/chr6/11044843-11044997.csv

This creates three files alongside the input:
  data/processed/chr6/11044843-11044997_train.csv
  data/processed/chr6/11044843-11044997_validation.csv
  data/processed/chr6/11044843-11044997_test.csv

Run split_csv.py for every marker you converted in Step 1.
Because assignments are shared in train_test_split.csv, the same sample tag
will always land in the same split across all markers.


────────────────────────────────────────
STEP 3A — Train a single-marker model
────────────────────────────────────────

  python3 train.py \
    -dp  data/processed/chr6/11044843-11044997_train.csv \
    -dpt data/processed/chr6/11044843-11044997_test.csv \
    -dpv data/processed/chr6/11044843-11044997_validation.csv \
    -m   ELOVL2_6

Supported single markers: ELOVL2_6, C1orf132, FHL2, CCDC102B

Optional flags:
  -lr   learning rate  (default 0.00003)
  -bs   batch size     (default 128)
  -e    max epochs     (default 1000)
  -d    dropout        (default 0.01)

The trained model is saved to models/new_predictor_<marker>_<input_size>.
Training stops automatically when validation loss stops improving (early stopping).


────────────────────────────────────────
STEP 3B — Train a multi-marker (concatenated) model
────────────────────────────────────────

First, concatenate the per-marker split files. Run concat_markers.py once per split,
passing MARKER=path pairs in the format MARKER_NAME=path/to/split.csv.

  python3 scripts/concat_markers.py \
    ELOVL2_6=data/processed/chr6/11044843-11044997_train.csv \
    C1orf132=data/processed/chr1/207996978-207997090_train.csv \
    -o data/processed/combined/ELOVL2_6_C1orf132_train.csv

  python3 scripts/concat_markers.py \
    ELOVL2_6=data/processed/chr6/11044843-11044997_validation.csv \
    C1orf132=data/processed/chr1/207996978-207997090_validation.csv \
    -o data/processed/combined/ELOVL2_6_C1orf132_validation.csv

  python3 scripts/concat_markers.py \
    ELOVL2_6=data/processed/chr6/11044843-11044997_test.csv \
    C1orf132=data/processed/chr1/207996978-207997090_test.csv \
    -o data/processed/combined/ELOVL2_6_C1orf132_test.csv

Each marker's pattern columns are prefixed with the marker name to avoid collisions.
A _manifest.json file is written alongside each CSV so augmentation runs independently
per marker and the results are combined in the correct format.
Only samples present in all markers are kept (inner join on tag).

Then train:

  python3 train.py \
    -dp  data/processed/combined/ELOVL2_6_C1orf132_train.csv \
    -dpt data/processed/combined/ELOVL2_6_C1orf132_test.csv \
    -dpv data/processed/combined/ELOVL2_6_C1orf132_validation.csv \
    -m   ELOVL2_6_C1orf132

The model input size and layer size are derived automatically from the manifest —
no extra flags are needed.


────────────────────────────────────────
STEP 4 — Predict
────────────────────────────────────────

  python3 predict.py \
    -m  ELOVL2_6 \
    -dp data/processed/chr6/11044843-11044997_test.csv

To save predictions to a CSV file:

  python3 predict.py \
    -m  ELOVL2_6 \
    -dp data/processed/chr6/11044843-11044997_test.csv \
    -s  True \
    -o  results/


────────────────────────────────────────
QUICK TEST with example data
────────────────────────────────────────

To verify the setup without preparing real data, run against the bundled examples:

  python3 train.py \
    -dp  example/elovl_example_train.csv \
    -dpt example/elovl_example_test.csv \
    -dpv example/elovl_example_validation.csv \
    -m   ELOVL2_6

  python3 predict.py \
    -m  ELOVL2_6 \
    -dp example/elovl_example_test.csv
